 Production Engineering OS owns contract compilation, meaningful-RED, implementation, sandboxed execution, verification of executable coverage, evidence, and the transition to `RELEASE_READY` or `HALTED`. A human owns release.
 
+## Verification gate
+
+Before returning an approved executable handoff, all checks must pass:
+
+1. The approved product-definition source has a known accountable human and no OPEN required decision; later product changes have renewed product approval.
+2. Every requested behavior maps to an `FR-*`; every requirement has an explicit criterion; every `AC-*` references existing requirements.
+3. Every acceptance intent maps without semantic change to exactly one admitted executable form. Actions, measures, operators, paths and trusted test/proof bindings are explicit and supported.
+4. Every binary release gate has its approved `acceptance_criterion_refs`, referencing existing criteria. A description alone never counts as an executable gate.
+5. The publisher's DRAFT and exact digest were shown to the accountable human; that human explicitly approved that digest before the approval publisher was called.
+6. `verify_contract_approval(contract, receipt, expected_approver=...)` verifies the exact complete published contract and expected identity; the canonical loader and current pinned compiler accept it unmodified.
+7. The documented receipt-bound engineering admission can accept those unchanged artifacts. Record its actual result separately from compilation; do not claim a build, release or live model run from admission.
+
+Unavailable publisher/compiler/admission support is BLOCKED with the missing prerequisite. Never mark compilation `ACCEPTED` because the tool was unavailable. A DRAFT review request is allowed before approval; it is not an executable handoff or a substitute for any check above.
+
+## Hard rules
+
+1. Never infer product intent, accountable identity, approval, gate references, executable bindings or expected values.
+2. Never hand-author a publisher contract or receipt digest. Use the existing canonical publisher; do not create a second serializer or approval authority.
+3. Never reuse approval after changing the approved meaning or generated bytes. Return to the affected approval boundary.
+4. Never relabel an unsupported product as the health example or invent a command, test, template proof or bundle interface to make it pass.
+5. Keep historical fixtures immutable. Current compatibility examples and TEST-ONLY issuance are separate from owner-approved artifacts and actual delivery evidence.
+
